1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,200
Five principles.

2
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:04,200
They're not magic, and they're not new.

3
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:08,914
They're the same five things that make any kind of communication

4
00:00:08,914 --> 00:00:11,057
work — but stated explicitly,

5
00:00:11,057 --> 00:00:15,771
because the AI won't fill in gaps the way a human

6
00:00:15,771 --> 00:00:16,971
collaborator does.

7
00:00:16,971 --> 00:00:18,171
Clarity.

8
00:00:18,171 --> 00:00:19,371
Context.

9
00:00:19,371 --> 00:00:20,571
Structure.

10
00:00:20,571 --> 00:00:21,771
Constraints.

11
00:00:21,771 --> 00:00:22,971
Examples.

12
00:00:22,971 --> 00:00:26,400
We'll go through each with a quick before-and-after,

13
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:29,400
and you'll start to see the pattern.

14
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:31,543
Once you internalize these five,

15
00:00:31,543 --> 00:00:36,257
almost every prompt-engineering technique will feel like a small twist on

16
00:00:36,257 --> 00:00:37,543
one of them.

17
00:00:37,543 --> 00:00:39,257
Principle one — clarity.

18
00:00:39,257 --> 00:00:40,457
Be specific.

19
00:00:40,457 --> 00:00:45,171
Watch what happens when I ask: "Help me with my email."

20
00:00:45,171 --> 00:00:46,886
That's not a prompt.

21
00:00:46,886 --> 00:00:51,600
That's the opening line of a conversation I haven't had yet.

22
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:56,314
The AI doesn't know what email, who it's to, what tone,

23
00:00:56,314 --> 00:00:58,029
what outcome I want.

24
00:00:58,029 --> 00:01:00,171
So it's going to guess.

25
00:01:00,171 --> 00:01:01,457
And guess wrong.

26
00:01:01,457 --> 00:01:06,171
Now look at this version: "Write a professional email to my

27
00:01:06,171 --> 00:01:09,171
client apologizing for a delayed project delivery.

28
00:01:09,171 --> 00:01:12,600
The delay was due to unexpected technical issues.

29
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:14,314
Maintain a positive tone,

30
00:01:14,314 --> 00:01:18,600
and propose a new deadline of next Friday." What changed?

31
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:21,171
I named the type of content.

32
00:01:21,171 --> 00:01:22,371
The recipient.

33
00:01:22,371 --> 00:01:23,571
The purpose.

34
00:01:23,571 --> 00:01:24,771
The reason.

35
00:01:24,771 --> 00:01:25,971
The tone.

36
00:01:25,971 --> 00:01:28,114
And the action I want.

37
00:01:28,114 --> 00:01:31,543
That's six pieces of information I added —

38
00:01:31,543 --> 00:01:35,400
and the result will be six times more useful.

39
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:39,257
There's a quick mental check I use for clarity.

40
00:01:39,257 --> 00:01:41,400
Read your prompt out loud.

41
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:46,114
If a contractor could deliver three completely different things and all

42
00:01:46,114 --> 00:01:49,543
of them would technically match what you wrote,

43
00:01:49,543 --> 00:01:51,686
the prompt isn't clear yet.

44
00:01:51,686 --> 00:01:56,400
Add the next constraint until only one shape of answer makes

45
00:01:56,400 --> 00:01:57,600
sense.

46
00:01:57,600 --> 00:02:02,314
The takeaway is simple: never assume the AI knows your context.

47
00:02:02,314 --> 00:02:03,514
State it.

48
00:02:03,514 --> 00:02:05,229
Principle two — context.

49
00:02:05,229 --> 00:02:08,229
Where clarity is about what you want,

50
00:02:08,229 --> 00:02:12,514
context is about why it matters and who it's for.

51
00:02:12,514 --> 00:02:14,229
Compare these two prompts.

52
00:02:14,229 --> 00:02:18,943
First: "Explain quantum computing." The AI gives you a wall of

53
00:02:18,943 --> 00:02:22,371
physics jargon — and depending on the model,

54
00:02:22,371 --> 00:02:27,086
it may be technically accurate but completely useless to your audience.

55
00:02:27,086 --> 00:02:31,800
Now: "I'm a high school teacher preparing a lesson for tenth

56
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:35,657
graders, who have basic knowledge of atoms and electricity.

57
00:02:35,657 --> 00:02:39,943
Explain quantum computing using simple analogies they can relate to.

58
00:02:39,943 --> 00:02:44,657
Keep it under three hundred words." Context tells the AI five

59
00:02:44,657 --> 00:02:45,857
things.

60
00:02:45,857 --> 00:02:47,571
Your role — teacher.

61
00:02:47,571 --> 00:02:49,714
Your audience — tenth graders.

62
00:02:49,714 --> 00:02:52,286
Their knowledge level — basic physics.

63
00:02:52,286 --> 00:02:54,429
The desired approach — analogies.

64
00:02:54,429 --> 00:02:57,857
And the constraints — under three hundred words.

65
00:02:57,857 --> 00:03:00,857
Think of context as setting the stage.

66
00:03:00,857 --> 00:03:05,143
The more relevant detail you give, the better the performance.

67
00:03:05,143 --> 00:03:08,571
And by relevant, I do mean relevant —

68
00:03:08,571 --> 00:03:11,571
don't pad your prompts with unnecessary backstory.

69
00:03:11,571 --> 00:03:13,714
Quality of context, not quantity.

70
00:03:13,714 --> 00:03:18,429
A two-sentence context line that actually tells the model who's reading

71
00:03:18,429 --> 00:03:23,143
and what they need is worth ten sentences of generic preamble.

72
00:03:23,143 --> 00:03:24,857
Principle three — structure.

73
00:03:24,857 --> 00:03:28,714
How you organize your prompt directly shapes the output.

74
00:03:28,714 --> 00:03:33,429
If you ask for a business plan in a single run-on

75
00:03:33,429 --> 00:03:36,429
sentence, you'll get a single run-on response.

76
00:03:36,429 --> 00:03:40,714
If you ask for one with named sections and lengths,

77
00:03:40,714 --> 00:03:44,571
you'll get something that looks like a real document.

78
00:03:44,571 --> 00:03:45,771
Watch this.

79
00:03:45,771 --> 00:03:50,486
The unstructured version: "I need a business plan for a coffee

80
00:03:50,486 --> 00:03:55,200
shop with financial projections and marketing strategy and competitive analysis." The

81
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:57,771
AI knows roughly what you want,

82
00:03:57,771 --> 00:04:02,057
but it has no scaffolding to hang the answer on.

83
00:04:02,057 --> 00:04:03,771
Now the structured version.

84
00:04:03,771 --> 00:04:06,771
I'm asking for the same thing —

85
00:04:06,771 --> 00:04:11,057
but I'm naming each section, listing the subsections under it,

86
00:04:11,057 --> 00:04:14,486
giving target word counts, and specifying bullet-point format.

87
00:04:14,486 --> 00:04:18,343
The AI now has a template to fill in,

88
00:04:18,343 --> 00:04:22,629
and the response comes back organized exactly like I asked.

89
00:04:22,629 --> 00:04:24,343
Rule of thumb —

90
00:04:24,343 --> 00:04:28,629
structure your prompt the way you'd want the output structured.

91
00:04:28,629 --> 00:04:32,486
If you want headers, use headers in the prompt.

92
00:04:32,486 --> 00:04:36,343
If you want a numbered list of five things,

93
00:04:36,343 --> 00:04:39,771
ask for a numbered list of five things.

94
00:04:39,771 --> 00:04:44,486
The shape of the prompt teaches the model the shape of

95
00:04:44,486 --> 00:04:45,686
the answer.

96
00:04:45,686 --> 00:04:49,543
Two principles in this slide, because they pair naturally.

97
00:04:49,543 --> 00:04:51,257
Principle four — constraints.

98
00:04:51,257 --> 00:04:52,971
Constraints don't limit creativity.

99
00:04:52,971 --> 00:04:54,257
They focus it.

100
00:04:54,257 --> 00:04:58,971
A prompt that says "write a story" gives you something generic.

101
00:04:58,971 --> 00:05:03,686
A prompt that says "write a 500-word science fiction story about

102
00:05:03,686 --> 00:05:08,400
a robot learning to paint, suitable for ages 8 to 12,

103
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:11,400
with a positive message about creativity" —

104
00:05:11,400 --> 00:05:14,829
that prompt gives you a much better story.

105
00:05:14,829 --> 00:05:16,543
The constraints aren't shackles.

106
00:05:16,543 --> 00:05:17,829
They're a frame.

107
00:05:17,829 --> 00:05:22,114
Useful constraints to remember: length, tone, format, audience, and style.

108
00:05:22,114 --> 00:05:25,543
Pick the ones that matter for your task.

109
00:05:25,543 --> 00:05:30,257
You don't need all five every time — but pick deliberately.

110
00:05:30,257 --> 00:05:31,971
Principle five — examples.

111
00:05:31,971 --> 00:05:35,829
When telling the AI what you want is hard,

112
00:05:35,829 --> 00:05:37,543
showing it is easier.

113
00:05:37,543 --> 00:05:41,829
If you've got a brand voice, paste in a sample.

114
00:05:41,829 --> 00:05:46,114
If you want a specific output format, demonstrate it once.

115
00:05:46,114 --> 00:05:50,829
If you want a tone you can't quite describe in words,

116
00:05:50,829 --> 00:05:54,686
give the AI a paragraph that has that tone.

117
00:05:54,686 --> 00:05:58,543
The model is incredibly good at pattern matching —

118
00:05:58,543 --> 00:06:02,829
give it one good pattern, and it will follow it.

119
00:06:02,829 --> 00:06:07,114
One good example often beats a thousand words of instruction.

120
00:06:07,114 --> 00:06:09,686
And — small but useful —

121
00:06:09,686 --> 00:06:13,543
the example you provide doesn't have to be perfect.

122
00:06:13,543 --> 00:06:17,829
It just has to be in the direction you want.

123
00:06:17,829 --> 00:06:20,829
The AI will smooth out the rest.

124
00:06:20,829 --> 00:06:22,114
That's all five.

125
00:06:22,114 --> 00:06:23,314
Clarity.

126
00:06:23,314 --> 00:06:24,514
Context.

127
00:06:24,514 --> 00:06:25,714
Structure.

128
00:06:25,714 --> 00:06:26,914
Constraints.

129
00:06:26,914 --> 00:06:28,114
Examples.

130
00:06:28,114 --> 00:06:32,400
You'll see them woven through every technique we cover next.

131
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:36,257
If you want to start applying them right now,

132
00:06:36,257 --> 00:06:40,114
open the learner workbook and start Task 1 —

133
00:06:40,114 --> 00:06:44,400
it's a real-world internal email scenario that uses all five.

134
00:06:44,400 --> 00:06:47,829
Or keep watching, and come back to it.

135
00:06:47,829 --> 00:06:49,543
In the next session,

136
00:06:49,543 --> 00:06:53,829
we'll layer eight practical techniques on top of these foundations.

137
00:06:53,829 --> 00:06:55,114
Let's go. ---
